tend to the case ν = 1, σ\nL10548@P134: 2 = ∞? What\nL10549@P134: happens when 0 6 ν < 1?\nL10550@P134: Problem 20.3. Extend this to the more general case of balls-in-boxes as in\nL10551@P134: Theorem 10.4. (We guess that the case 0 < λ < ν is easy by the methods in\nL10552@P134: the references above, in particular [55] and [33, Section 3.2.1], but we have\nL10553@P134: not checked the details.)\nL10554@P134: Problem 20.4. Extend this to the subtree counts in Theorem 7.12.\nL10555@P134: 20.3. Height and width. We have studied the random trees Tn without\nL10556@P134: any scaling. Since our mode of convergence really means that we consider\nL10557@P134: only a finite number of generations at a time, we are really looking at the\nL10558@P134: base of the tree, with the first generations. The results in this paper thus\nL10559@P134: do not say anything about, for example, the height and width of Tn. (Recall\nL10560@P134: that if T is a rooted tree, then the height H(T) := max{k : lk(T) > 0}, the\nL10561@P134: maximum distance from the root, and the width W(T) := maxk{lk(T)}, the\nL10562@P134: largest size of a generation.) However, there are other known results.\nL10563@P134: In the case ν > 1, σ\nL10564@P134: 2 < ∞ (the case Iα in Section 8), it is well-known that\nL10565@P134: both the height H(Tn) and the width W(Tn) of Tn typically are of order √\nL10566@P134: n;\nL10567@P134: more precisely,\nL10568@P134: H(Tn)/\nL10569@P134: √\nL10570@P134: n\nL10571@P134: d\nL10572@P134: −→ 2σ\nL10573@P134: −1X, (20.10)\nL10574@P134: W(Tn)/\nL10575@P134: √\nL10576@P134: n\nL10577@P134: d\nL10578@P134: −→ σX, (20.11)\nL10579@P134: where X is some strictly positive random variable (in fact, X equals the\nL10580@P134: maximum of a standard Brownian excursion and has what is known as a\nL10581@P134: theta distribution), see e.g. Kolchin [76], Aldous [4], Chassaing, Marckert\nL10582@P134: and Yor [25], Janson [59] and Drmota [33]. There are also results for a\nL10583@P134: single lev