w, but not on x or n)?\nL10637@P135: It follows from (20.10)–(20.11) and (20.12)–(20.13) that E H(Tn)/\nL10638@P135: √\nL10639@P135: n and\nL10640@P135: E W(Tn)/\nL10641@P135: √\nL10642@P135: n converge to positive numbers. (In fact, the limits are √2π/σ\nL10643@P135: and p\nL10644@P135: π/2 σ, see e.g. Janson [61], where also joint moments are computed.)\nL10645@P135: Problem 20.10. What are the growth rates of E H(Tn) and E W(Tn) when\nL10646@P135: σ\nL10647@P135: 2 = ∞ or ν < 1?\nL10648@P135: 20.4. Scaled trees. The results (20.10)–(20.11), as well as many other re\u0002sults on various asymptotics of Tn in the case ν > 1, σ\nL10649@P135: 2 < ∞, can be seen\nL10650@P135: as consequences of the convergence of the tree Tn, after rescaling in a suit\u0002able sense in both height and width by √\nL10651@P135: n, to the continuum random tree\nL10652@P135: defined by Aldous [3, 4, 5], see also Le Gall [80]. (The continuum random\nL10653@P135: tree is not an ordinary tree; it is a compact metric space.) This has been\nL10654@P135: extended to the case σ\nL10655@P135: 2 = ∞ when π is in the domain of attraction of a\nL10656@P135: stable distribution, see e.g. Duquesne [34] and Le Gall [80, 81]; the limit is\nL10657@P135: now a different random metric space called a stable tree.\nL10658@P135: Problem 20.11. Is there some kind of similar limiting object in the case\nL10659@P135: ν < 1 (after suitable scaling)?\nL10660@P135: 20.5. Random walks. Simple random walk on the infinite random tree Tb\nL10661@P135: has been studied by many authors in the critical case ν > 1, in particular\nL10662@P135: when σ\nL10663@P135: 2 < ∞, see e.g. Kesten [74], Barlow and Kumagai [9], Durhuus,\nL10664@P135: Jonsson and Wheater [35], Fujii and Kumagai [43], but also when σ\nL10665@P135: 2 = ∞,\nL10666@P135-136: see Croydon and Kumagai [30] (assuming attraction to a stable law).SIMPLY GENERATED TREES AND RANDOM ALLOCATIONS 137\nL10667@P136: A different approach is to study simple random walk on Tn and study\nL10668@P136: asymptotics os n → ∞. For example, by rescaling the tree one can obtain\nL10669@P136: convergence to a process on the continuum random tree (when σ\nL10670@P136: 2 < ∞) or\nL10671@P136: stable tree (assuming attraction to a stable law), see Croydon [28, 29].\nL10672@P136: For ν < 1, the simple random walk on Tb does not make sense, since the\nL10673@P136: tree has a node with infinite degree. Nevertheless, it might be interesting\nL10674@P136: to study simple random walk on Tn and find asymptotics of interesting\nL10675@P136: quantities as n → ∞.\nL10676@P136: 20.6. Multi-type conditioned Galton–Watson trees. It seems likely\nL10677@P136: that there are results similar to the ones in Section 7 for multi-type Galton–\nL10678@P136: Watson trees conditioned on the total size, or perhaps on the number of\nL10679@P136: nodes of each type, and for corresponding generalizations of simply gener\u0002ated random trees. We are not aware of any such results, however, and leave\nL10680@P136: this as an open problem. (See Kurtz, Lyons, Pemantle and Peres [78] for\nL10681@P136: related results that presumably are useful.)\nL10682@P136: 21. Different conditionings for Galton–Watson trees\nL10683@P136: One of the principal objects studied in this paper is the conditioned\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1812.07365)\nciteturn246view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 25; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn245view1\",\"pattern\":\"Conjecture\"}); Total lines: 3756\nL3653@P22: fixed c > 0 and β > 2, and denote by Tn a µ-GW tree conditioned to have n vertices. Let k ≥ 1\nL3654@P22: and A1, A2, . . . , Ak ⊂ Z+ be finite. Then we have the joint convergence in distribution\nL3655@P22: \u0012\nL3656@P22: N A1 (Tn√\nL3657@P22: ) − nµA1\nL3658@P22: n\nL3659@P22: , . . . ,\nL3660@P22: N Ak (Tn√) − nµAk\nL3661@P22: n\nL3662@P22: \u0013\nL3663@P22: −→ (ZA1, . . . , ZAk),\nL3664@P22: where ZAi ∼ N (0, µAi(1 − µAi)) and for i 6= j:\nL3665@P22: Cov(ZAi, ZAj) = µAi∩Aj − µAiµAj.\nL3666@P22: Proof. By [7, Theorem 1] (see [23, Sec. 2.1] for its use in this context) or [16, Theorem 19.34]\nL3667@P22: after removing the largest outdegree in Tn, the other outdegrees are asymptotically i.i.d. with\nL3668@P22: distribution µ. Therefore, for every M ≥ 1, the law of the vector N{1}(Tn), . . . , N{M}(Tn)\nL3669@P22: \u0001\nL3670@P22: is\nL3671@P22: asymptotically multinomial with parameters (n, µ1, . . . , µM). The result follows.\nL3672@P22: Conjecture. We have seen that the conclusions of Theorem 6.7 hold for µ with infinite variance\nL3673@P22: in the domain of attraction of a stable law and for µ a subcritical power law. We believe that these\nL3674@P22: conclusions should hold for any µ critical with infinite variance, as well as for µ subcritical with\nL3675@P22: no exponential moment. In particular, we should get, for any A ⊂ Z+, (N A(Tn) − nµA)/\nL3676@P22: √\nL3677@P22: n\nL3678@P22: d\nL3679@P22: →\nL3680@P22: N (0, µA (1 − µA)). However, in the general case, nothing is known about the scaling limits of\nL3681@P22: such GW trees (see [16] for detailed arguments and counterexamples) and no general local limit\nL3682@P22: theorem exists, which prevents us from directly generalizing our methods.\nL3683@P22: References\nL3684@P22: [1] R. Abraham and J.-F. Delmas. Local limits of conditioned Galton-Watson trees: the con\u0002densation case. Electron. J. Probab., 19:no. 56, 29, 2014.\nL3685@P22: [2] R. Abraham and J.-F. Delmas. Local limits of conditioned Galton-Watson trees: the infinite\nL3686@P22: spine case. Electron. J. Probab., 19:no. 2, 19, 2014.\nL3687@P22: [3] L. Addario-Berry. A probabilistic approach to block sizes in random maps. Preprint available\nL3688@P22: on arxiv, arXiv:1503.08159, 2015.\nL3689@P22: [4] D. Aldous. The continuum random tree. I. Ann. Probab., 19(1):1–28, 1991.\nL3690@P22-23: 23[5] D. Aldo